    {
      "@id": "evidence:lawrence-human-machine-communication-constraint",
      "@type": "Evidence",
      "label": "Lawrence contrasts human and machine communication rates without equating rate with understanding",
      "statement": "Lawrence uses Jean-Dominique Bauby, who composed text through eyelid blinks after locked-in syndrome, to illustrate intelligence constrained by an extremely narrow communication channel. His lecture notes estimate about 6 bits per minute for Bauby, about 2,000 bits per minute for ordinary speech, and billions of bits per minute for computer communication. He generalises that human intelligence is relatively 'locked in' while also arguing that machines lack the evolved, embodied, contextual understanding involved in human communication. The comparison supports a communication-bottleneck premise; it does not establish that greater machine throughput transfers a person's complete knowledge or produces shared understanding.",
      "status": "authoritative-source",
      "review_status": "source-verified",
      "origin": "external-source",
      "evidence_level": "direct-source",
      "audiences": [
