    {
      "@id": "example:framework-self-efficiency-evaluation",
      "@type": "Example",
      "label": "Self-evaluation of the Framework's document-equivalent and task context efficiency",
      "statement": "A proposed controlled self-evaluation freezes one Framework Semantic Artifact snapshot and derives a semantic-unit inventory covering objects, properties, relationships, provenance, epistemic status, qualifications, counterarguments, history and explicit gaps. A conventional comprehensive document corpus is generated and revised until it passes declared coverage and fidelity thresholds. Held-out tasks then compare at least four conditions: a concise README-style document, the fidelity-constrained comprehensive document corpus, the full serialized Semantic Artifact, and bounded retrieval from the Semantic Artifact. Tasks are stratified across direct fact retrieval, multi-hop relationships, provenance and change history, disagreement and uncertainty, missing-knowledge recognition, and cross-object synthesis. Each condition is measured for semantic answer accuracy, source-trace precision and recall, qualification and contradiction recovery, unsupported additions, input and output tokens, model-visible tool tokens, latency, construction and maintenance effort, and update propagation. Repeated trials, controlled ordering and an independent or blinded evaluator reduce generator and evaluator coupling. The experiment supports a benefit only if the Semantic Artifact condition meets the quality and fidelity gates and shows a favourable task-time or amortized result; a larger full representation or a negative break-even result remains a valid finding.",
